@meta
id: 12-01
title: The key you already use, done properly
chapter: 12
type: lesson
skills: search-slash
par: 21
minutes: 4
vscode: Ctrl+F, type it, Enter, click back into the file
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
A hundred and thirty lines of provider, and one error code in it is
spelled wrong. Sentry has been swallowing it for a month because the code
does not exist in the registry.

    ORDER_NOT_FUND   should be   ORDER_NOT_FOUND

It is in `cancel`, near the bottom. You could scroll. You could work out
the line number. Both are slower than saying what you are looking for.

This is the first idea of the chapter and it is a small one: vim can jump
straight to text. The second idea, which arrives in a few lessons, is the
one that matters — in vim, searching is not just looking, it is moving,
and anything that moves can be attached to an edit.

Fix the spelling and save.

@keys
| `/text` `Enter` | jump forward to the next match of `text` |
| `Esc` | while typing a search, abandon it and stay where you were |
| `u` | undo, as always, if the jump lands you somewhere surprising |

@hints
Do not move the cursor by hand at all. Ask vim for the text instead —
there is a key that opens a search prompt at the bottom of the screen.
--
`/` starts a forward search. Type what you want and press Enter. The
cursor lands on the first character of the match.
--
You do not have to search for the whole word. `/FUND` is enough to be
unique in this file, and the cursor lands on the `F`. From there it is
one change-word away: `cw`, type the right spelling, `Esc`.

@solution
/FUND<Enter>  cwFOUND<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Eighteen keystrokes, and eleven of them are the two words involved.

Two things worth noticing. First, you searched for `FUND`, not
`ORDER_NOT_FUND`. A search is not a name you have to get right — it is
the shortest string that is unique enough, and you can see whether you
guessed well because the cursor is already sitting on the answer.

Second, the cursor is now *at* the mistake, not near it. That is the
difference between vim's search and the find box in an editor. Ctrl+F in
VS Code shows you a match and leaves your cursor where it was; you then
click, or press Escape and lose the match. In vim the search *is* the
move. `cw` works immediately because you are already there.

If a search lands you somewhere you did not want, `Ctrl-O` takes you back
to where you jumped from — that is chapter 17, and searching is the main
reason you will want it.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../customer/customer.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(args: { id: string }) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id: args.id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async findByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({
      where: { id: args.customerId },
    });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId: customer.id });
  }

  public async create(args: { customerId: string; total: number }) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({
      where: { id: args.customerId },
    });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    const order = await this.orderRepository.create({
      values: {
        customerId: customer.id,
        total: args.total,
        status: 'PENDING',
      },
    });

    await this.emailService.sendOrderConfirmation(customer.email, order.id);

    return order;
  }

  public async addLine(args: { id: string; sku: string; price: number }) {
    const order = await this.findOne({ id: args.id });

    if (order.status !== 'PENDING') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SUBMITTED');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id: order.id },
      values: { total: order.total + args.price },
    });
  }

  public async applyDiscount(args: { id: string; percent: number }) {
    const order = await this.findOne({ id: args.id });

    if (args.percent < 0 || args.percent > 100) {
      throw exception('DISCOUNT_OUT_OF_RANGE');
    }

    const discounted = Math.round(order.total * (1 - args.percent / 100));

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id: order.id },
      values: { total: discounted },
    });
  }

  public async submit(args: { id: string }) {
    const order = await this.findOne({ id: args.id });

    if (order.total <= 0) {
      throw exception('ORDER_EMPTY');
    }

    await this.paymentService.charge(order.id, order.total);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id: order.id },
      values: { status: 'SUBMITTED' },
    });
  }

  public async ship(args: { id: string; carrier: string }) {
    const order = await this.findOne({ id: args.id });

    if (order.status !== 'SUBMITTED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_SUBMITTED');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id: order.id },
      values: { status: 'SHIPPED', carrier: args.carrier },
    });
  }

  public async cancel(args: { id: string }) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id: args.id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FUND');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id: order.id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../customer/customer.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
